    "available_at": "2026-11-20T21:41:12.500925+00:00",
    "body": "View of a rig drilling the Makoko District in the Western Forelands. ( Image courtesy of Ivanhoe Mines’ Presentation )\nBy: Henry Lazenby November 20, 2026\nFewer mining projects moved closer to production in the quarter to Sept. 30, even as companies reported a record number of drill holes, S&P Global says.\nMining companies raised $9.81 billion (C$14.1 billion) in the quarter to Sept. 30, 28% less than the $13.6 billion they raised in the quarter to June 30, the consultant reported Wednesday. A drop in borrowing outweighed a rise in share sales.\n“A shortage of metal does not begin when the market physically runs out of material,” Patricia Barreto, an associate director at S&P Global Energy, said during a webinar. “It begins several years earlier when good projects fail to move quickly enough from discovery to resource, from resource to feasibility and from feasibility into construction.”\nS&P counted nine development milestones in the quarter to Sept. 30, down from 16 in the quarter to June 30 and 23 a year earlier. Five mines opened and construction began on two others. The drilling rebound could lead to more initial resource estimates and more projects advancing in 2027 if financing holds up, S&P said. The mining companies it tracks gained 6% in market value to $3.95 trillion, a record in its quarterly figures.\nGold leads drilling\nCompanies reported results from 18,474 drill holes, up 59% from the quarter to June 30 and the most in S&P’s series. The number of projects reporting results rose 36% to 718, the most since the quarter to March 31, 2023. Gold accounted for nearly six in 10. Silver accounted for 67 projects, more than double the number a year earlier. Copper rose 11% over the same period to 88 projects. Australia led with 230 projects, followed by Canada with 164 and the United States with 78.\nThe number of early-stage projects reporting drill results rose 51% from the quarter to June 30. They accounted for 42% of all projects reporting results. A separate S&P budget survey put global nonferrous exploration budgets at $16.5 billion this year, up a third, led by gold and copper. Canada’s budget rose 37% to $3.14 billion, largely on gold.\nDrill results were the only component of S&P’s Pipeline Activity Index to improve. The index, which also tracks initial resources, financings and milestones, gained 5% from the quarter to June 30 but remained 14% below a year earlier. S&P counted 49 development milestones in the first nine months of 2026, close to the 51 it tracked in the same period last year.\nFrom drill to mine\nThe only completed feasibility study in S&P’s tally came from Liberty Gold (TSX: LGD; US-OTC: LGDTF) at its Black Pine gold project in southeastern Idaho. The September study estimated a 16-year open-pit mine would cost $411 million to build. Liberty aims to finish detailed engineering in the third quarter of 2027 and start construction in the first half of 2028, subject to permits and a formal decision to build.\nMiners announced eight initial resource estimates, down from 13 a year earlier. Two deposits accounted for most of the gold and copper in those estimates. At the Apollo deposit in Colombia, Collective Mining (TSX, Nasdaq: CNL) reported an indicated resource of 37 million tonnes at 1.38 grams gold per tonne for 1.64 million oz. and an inferred resource of 48.2 million tonnes at 1.6 grams for 2.47 million ounces. The estimate, effective Aug. 31, accounted for about two-thirds of the 6.2 million oz. of gold S&P tallied, including byproduct gold.\nIn Nevada, Lion Copper (CSE, Nasdaq: LCU) reported on Aug. 4 that the Bear deposit held an indicated resource of 1.06 billion tonnes at 0.29% copper for 3.04 million tonnes of the metal. An inferred resource of 1.77 billion tonnes at 0.22% contained 3.98 million tonnes. Bear accounted for about 91% of the 7.7 million tonnes of copper in the quarter’s initial estimates, including byproduct copper.\nEquity overtakes debt\nDebt financing fell 54% from the quarter to June 30, bringing total fundraising to its lowest level since the quarter to Dec. 31, 2024. Share sales accounted for 59% of the money miners raised, up from 36% the previous quarter. In September, the U.S. Federal Reserve raised its benchmark rate by a quarter of a percentage point.\nGeneration Mining (TSX: GENM; US-OTC: GENMF) raised C$240 million through a bought deal and a private placement with Canada Growth Fund to help build its Marathon copper-palladium project in northwestern Ontario. Generation began construction in October.\nMajors raised 65% less than in the quarter to June 30, while juniors raised 8% less. A rebound in financing by mid-tier miners lifted the combined total for juniors and mid-tiers by 14% to $7.05 billion. The number of significant financings for the two groups fell to 146 from 272 a year earlier.\nAlcoa (NYSE: AA; ASX: AAI) raised $2.6 billion through two subsidiaries to help buy bauxite, alumina and aluminum assets from South32 (ASX, LSE: S32). The $1.5-billion bond issued by Alcoa subsidiary Alumina Pty Ltd. was the largest financing S&P tracked in the quarter. Mid-tier producer Wanguo Gold Group (HKEX: 3939) raised HK$5.5 billion (about $700 million) through convertible bonds to pursue gold mine acquisitions overseas.\nWhere capital went\nCanadian projects drew the most financing at $1.77 billion. Financing for Latin American projects rose 56% from the quarter to June 30 to $1.63 billion. S&P’s regional tally excludes debt issues above $500 million.\nS&P tracked 49 mining deals worth at least $5 million each. Gold accounted for 22 and copper for 16. Across all commodities, 37 deals involved assets that weren’t producing.\nInvestors favour producing mines and advanced projects, S&P Global Energy associate director Nick Trickett said. Further rate increases and swings in metal prices would leave juniors especially exposed as they try to raise money, he said.\nPrint\nCOMMODITY: Copper Gold Nickel Silver\nREGION: Africa Asia Australia, NZ & South Pacific Canada Europe South America United States\nCOMPANY: S&P Global\nRepublish this article",
